@page "/segmented"

<CatHead Title="Segmented control">
    <code>.segmented</code> changes a <em>setting</em> in place &mdash; a range, a layout, a unit
    &mdash; from a few short options. If there is a panel of content below it, it is
    <a href="/tabs">tabs</a>; if the options are many or long, it is a
    <a href="/form-select">select</a>.
</CatHead>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Segmented.Segmented" Title="Segmented control" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        Radios under the hood, so arrow keys move between the options and the chosen one is
        announced as chosen, with no ARIA at all. Give every input the same <code>name</code>, and
        give the group a name with a <code>&lt;fieldset&gt;</code> or
        <code>aria-labelledby</code>, or a screen reader announces three loose radios. Reach for it
        instead of a <code>&lt;select&gt;</code> when the options are few and short.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Segmented.Sizes" Title="Three sizes" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.segmented--sm</code> and <code>.segmented--lg</code> take the same
        <code>--control-height</code> tiers as <a href="/button"><code>.btn-sm</code></a> and
        <a href="/form-text"><code>.form-input-sm</code></a>, so a segmented control in a bar is the same
        height as the field and the button beside it; the bare class is the middle step. The tier
        goes on the <em>track</em>, not on the options.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Segmented.InAToolbar" Title="In a toolbar" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        Two of them in one bar: a range inside <code>.toolbar-filters</code>, because it narrows
        what is listed, and a layout switch at the far end, because it changes how the same list is
        drawn. It still needs a group name — <code>aria-label</code> on the
        <code>.segmented</code> is enough here, where a <code>&lt;fieldset&gt;</code> would add a
        border and a legend the bar has no room for.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<div class="callout callout-info">
    <i class="ri-question-line"></i>
    <div class="callout-body">
        <span class="callout-title">Not a way to group actions</span>
        Two or more buttons that <em>do</em> something to the same thing are a
        <a href="/button-group">button group</a>. A segmented control is announced as a choice, and
        pressing an option must change nothing but the setting it names.
    </div>
</div>
